# FIXED DEFECT 062, found by panel 162's compiler-engineer 2026-09-18, narrowed
# and repaired at M-readable-bytes' close the same day.
#
# SYMPTOM: a field declared `cstr` where the header has `char[N]` reported
# `ffi_incomplete_record: does not name nodename` — with `nodename` declared on
# the line below — and never reported the wrong type at all. The author was sent
# to add a field they had already written, while the real fault stayed silent.
# design.md §4.17 inverted twice in one message.
#
# CAUSE, and it is one word. `emit/extern_field.hero`'s SIX branches each write
# a marker clang echoes back on failure, `heroes-ffi-field <record> <member>`,
# and `emit/ffi_field.field_type` reads exactly two words out of it. Five
# branches wrote the HEROES record's name. The `cstr` branch wrote `c_type` —
# which for a TAGGED record is two words, `struct tagged` — so the reader took
# `struct` for the record and `tagged` for the member, found no such record, and
# returned nothing. clang had said the true thing twice, `256 == 8` on both
# fields; the only diagnostic that survived was the completeness probe's.
#
# FIX: the sixth branch writes `hero_name` like the other five.
#
# WHY THE HEADER IS TAGGED. An untagged `typedef struct {…} Tag;` gives a
# one-word `c_type`, so the marker parses by accident and the defect does not
# fire. The tag is what makes this case a test rather than a decoration.
#
# THE SHAPE BESIDE IT, and it is why this file is in `unsupported/` and not
# `fixedbugs/`: with the field types CORRECT, `ffi_incomplete_record` is right
# and stays right — measured at the close, two of five fields declared, and it
# names `release`, which is genuinely the third and genuinely undeclared. This
# case does not touch that.

extern "ffi-a-cstr-field-blames-the-field.h"
    record Tagged tag tagged
        name: cstr  #~ ffi_field_type
        id: i32
    function tagged_id(t: Tagged) -> i32

function main()
    t = Tagged(name: "x".cstr(), id: 7)
    print(t.tagged_id().to_i64().must())
